const toc = [
  { label: 'The protocol surface', href: '#protocol' },
  { label: 'The connection', href: '#connection' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '#tools' },
  { label: 'Sub-agents', href: '#subagents' },
  { label: 'Reaching the client\u2019s files', href: '#fs' },
  { label: 'Direct prompt mode', href: '#direct' },
  { label: 'Patched cersei', href: '#cersei' },
]

// Tuples, not objects: Panda scans `src/` for style-shaped objects, and object
// keys that happen to be CSS properties become bogus declarations.
const methods: [string, string][] = [
  ['initialize', 'Advertises capabilities and the authentication methods the client may use.'],
  ['session/new', 'Starts a session with a working directory and, optionally, extra MCP servers. It also advertises the available providers and models so a client can switch models mid-session.'],
  ['session/load', 'Resumes an existing session, replaying the conversation as session/update notifications.'],
  ['session/prompt', 'Streams session/update notifications for a turn and finishes with a stop reason.'],
  ['session/set_config_option', 'Changes a session option, alongside session/set_mode and unstable_set_session_model.'],
  ['session/cancel', 'Cancels the turn in progress.'],
]

const connectionMethods: [string, string][] = [
  ['send, send_line', 'Serialize a value, or a raw line, onto stdout.'],
  ['response, error', 'A successful JSON-RPC response, or an error one.'],
  ['notification, send_update', 'A JSON-RPC notification, and the session/update form of it.'],
  ['send_available_commands', 'Tells the client which slash commands exist.'],
]

const subAgentFields: [string, string][] = [
  ['id, display_name', 'How the parent and the transcript refer to it.'],
  ['spawner_prompt', 'What the parent model is told about the sub-agent, which is how it knows when to spawn it.'],
  ['system_prompt', 'The sub-agent\u2019s own instructions.'],
  ['tools', 'Builds a fresh tool set for each spawned instance rather than sharing one.'],
  ['max_turns', 'Caps how long a sub-agent may run.'],
  ['include_message_history', 'Whether the sub-agent is seeded with the parent\u2019s conversation.'],
  ['strip_think_tags', 'Whether reasoning tags are stripped from its output before the parent sees it.'],
]

const compact = `AUTOCOMPACT_TRIGGER_FRACTION = 0.90
KEEP_RECENT_MESSAGES        = 10
MAX_CONSECUTIVE_FAILURES    = 3`
---

<Docs
  title="Agent runtime"
  description="How the agent side actually runs: the protocol the desktop app speaks to it, the tools it has, how it delegates to sub-agents, and the patches carried on the vendored agent library."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    One agent implementation serves both front ends. <code>apps/agent-cli</code> is a terminal
    TUI that doubles as a protocol server; the desktop app's agent pane is a client of that same
    protocol. This page is the implementation side. For connecting your own provider or tool
    servers, see <a href="/docs/configuration">Configuration</a>, and for the sync and provider
    layer, <a href="/docs/contributor/integrations">Integrations</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="protocol">The protocol surface</h2>
  <p>
    Running <code>agent-cli</code> with <code>--acp</code> makes it an Agent Client Protocol
    server over stdin and stdout: newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0, implementing a subset of the
    Gemini CLI's ACP v1 surface. Requests arrive on stdin; responses and streaming notifications
    leave on stdout.
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="ACP methods">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Method</th><th>Does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {methods.map(([method, detail]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{method}</code></td>
            <td>{detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The direction of travel is not one-way. The agent can send its own requests back to the
    client — <code>fs/read_text_file</code> and <code>fs/write_text_file</code> — and awaits the
    responses, correlating them by id. Those calls are gated on what the client says it supports
    at initialization.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fallback switch is reported to the client rather than happening silently, so a model that
    quietly changed mid-session cannot be mistaken for the one you chose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connection">The connection</h2>
  <p>
    <code>AcpConnection</code> owns the wire. Its job is twofold: every write to stdout is
    serialized, so a response and a streaming notification produced by different tasks can never
    interleave into a corrupt line; and requests the agent has sent to the client are tracked by
    id so their answers can be matched up.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>Does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {connectionMethods.map(([method, detail]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{method}</code></td>
          <td>{detail}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the built-in tool set, the agent carries tools of its own.{' '}
    <code>AskUserTool</code> is the one worth knowing about, because it has two modes: in the TUI
    it puts the question to you and waits for the answer, and under ACP or a headless run it
    returns a structured result instead. That is what lets the same interview flow be driven
    from a terminal, from the desktop pane, or from a script without a second implementation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its input schema covers the question, an optional short header, suggested answers, a
    multi-select flag, and validation rules — the same shape the app's own question UI uses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="subagents">Sub-agents</h2>
  <p>
    Delegation is a tool: <code>spawn_agents</code> hands focused work to sub-agents that have
    their own system prompts and their own tools. The parent's system prompt lists the catalog,
    with a short note per sub-agent on when to reach for it, so spawning is a decision the model
    makes rather than something hardcoded around it.
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="A sub-agent definition">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {subAgentFields.map(([field, detail]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{field}</code></td>
            <td>{detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    Sub-agent activity is broadcast on a channel rather than being flattened into the parent's
    output. The TUI subscribes and renders nested tool calls underneath the{' '}
    <code>spawn_agents</code> call that caused them, which is what makes delegation legible
    instead of a black box. Under ACP and headless runs there is no receiver, and those events
    are simply dropped.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fs">Reaching the client's files</h2>
  <p>
    The file tools go through a trait rather than straight to disk, so a client can serve
    unsaved editor buffers and observe the edits the agent makes. In the TUI and in headless
    runs there is no client to ask, so the sink is absent and the tools touch the local
    filesystem directly. The capability is negotiated, not assumed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="direct">Direct prompt mode</h2>
  <p>
    Not everything needs the agent loop. Direct prompt mode sends a single user message to one
    model and returns the raw streamed response, resolving the provider, model, and key through
    the same machinery as the rest of the CLI.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its real purpose is testing: it captures exactly what a model streams, including the thinking
    deltas that the provider layer would otherwise drop, and saves them as fixtures organised by
    format family and model. Parsing can then be tested offline against real responses instead
    of hand-written approximations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cersei">Patched cersei</h2>
  <p>
    The agent is built on the cersei agent library, and the repository carries patched copies of
    its agent and provider crates. The patches exist because the behaviour the app needs is in
    the library's internals rather than behind an interface.
  </p>
  <p>
    The largest is auto-compaction: as a conversation approaches the model's context limit, older
    messages are summarized to make room while keeping the recent thread intact.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="patched/cersei-agent/src/compact.rs" code={compact} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    Compaction triggers at 90% of the window, always preserves the ten most recent messages, and
    disables itself after three consecutive failures rather than looping on a conversation it
    cannot shrink. A conversation's fullness is also surfaced to the UI as a three-state warning,
    so running out of context is something you can see coming.
  </p>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Read the patches as part of the codebase">
    Anything in <code>patched/</code> is code you maintain. A bug in agent behaviour may live
    there rather than in <code>apps/agent-cli</code>, and an upstream update has to be
    reconciled with the patches rather than merged over them.
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/contributor/workflow-engine">The workflow engine</a> explains the coding
    pipeline that drives these sessions, and{' '}
    <a href="/docs/contributor/integrations">Integrations</a> covers the provider layer and the
    MCP servers you configure yourself.
  </p>
</Docs>
